.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Libadraw
holds helpers for a graphical program that looks and works as
.IR acme (1)
does, in a window of its own, as
.IR chess (1)
is to.
A program uses those it wants; none of them needs the others,
and none opens a window or reads its events.
.PP
A
.B Scroll
is a scroll bar that looks and works as acme's own.
.B r
is where it is drawn,
.B tot
the size of what it scrolls, in any unit,
and
.B p0
and
.B p1
the part of it that shows.
.I Scrollinit
gives it acme's colors from the theme
.RI ( theme (6)):
.BR body 's
back, or acme's pale yellow, for the part that shows and
.B bodyborder
for the rest.
.I Scrolldraw
draws it in
.IR dst .
.I Scrollwid
and
.I Scrollgap
are acme's width for a scroll bar and the gap after it, scaled for the display.
.PP
.I Scrollmouse
works the bar while a button pressed in it is held, as acme does:
button 1 moves back and button 3 forward, by as much of what shows as the pointer is
down the bar; button 2 moves to the place the pointer is at; and buttons 1 and 3 repeat
while held, after a pause.
Each time it moves,
.I scrollmouse
sets
.B p0
and
.B p1
and calls
.IR set ,
with
.IR a ,
to draw what shows from
.BR p0 .
The program moves for the mouse wheel itself.
.SS Tags
A
.B Tag
is a tag drawn and worked as acme's, at the top of a program's window, for a program
that draws what acme can't. The tag's text and its selection are the tag's, as acme's are
acme's, and the program learns of what the user does in it by events, as an
.IR acme (4)
client does, with the calls and the
.B Event
of
.IR acme (2).
Its calls are those a file server of tags would serve, and
.I tagpipe
is a library function either way;
the text below names the files as they would be, in
.BR /mnt/adraw/ n.
.PP
.I Tagopen
opens a tag on the program's window,
.BR screen ,
with the mouse and keyboard
.I mc
and
.IR kc
the program has made, and the text
.I name
and then
.BR "Del Snarf |" ,
where the program adds its words, as acme's clients do.
From then on the tag reads
.I mc
and
.IR kc ,
keeps what falls on it, the mouse in it and the keys while the pointer was last in it,
and hands the rest on
.B t->mc
and
.BR t->kc ,
which the program reads as it would its own, and which a server's window would give it as
.B /dev/mouse
and
.BR /dev/cons .
The tag also takes rio's resizes: it calls
.I getwindow
(see
.IR graphics (2)),
draws itself again, sets
.BR body ,
the part of the window under the tag that is the program's, and sends on
.BR t->mc->resizec ,
on which the program draws its body again.
The tag sets
.B body
before
.I tagopen
returns, and changes too when the tag's lines do.
The tag draws in acme's tag colors from the theme
.RI ( theme (6)),
read when it opens, or in those its
.B ctl
gives.
The program doesn't call
.I getwindow
itself, which would draw over the tag, and draws from a proc other than the one that
opened the tag only with the display locked
.RI ( lockdisplay
in
.IR graphics (2)).
A gesture, from the press of a button to the release of the last, belongs to where it
began: one begun in the body goes to the program even where it passes over the tag, and
the program sees the pointer move over the tag with no button down.
The program may call the tag's functions from any proc: edits of the tag that come while
the user is working in it wait until the gesture ends.
.I Tagclose
stops the tag: it no longer reads the mouse and keyboard, frees what it drew with, and
refuses later calls.
.PP
In the tag, as in acme's: button 1 selects, a double click selects a word or the text between
brackets or quotes, and the 1-2 and 1-3 chords cut and paste by
.BR /dev/snarf ,
which acme shares;
typing replaces the selection, backspace and control-U and -W erase, control-A and -E
go to the ends of the line, control-X, -C and -V cut, copy and paste, Escape selects what the user
typed since the last click, or cuts the selection, and control-F completes a file name, as
.IR complete (2)
does for acme.
The down arrow and a newline show all the tag's lines, and the up arrow or the wheel one.
The square at the tag's left works as acme's on its window, by rio's
.B wctl
.RI ( rio (4)),
each time within rio's screen as it is then, border and all, in a rectangle rio's
.B goodrect
takes, and with what rio refuses reported:
a click of button 1 makes the window taller by acme's step, until it fills the screen's height,
button 1 dragged moves it, once, where the button comes up,
button 2 gives it the screen's height, and button 3 makes it fill the screen, as near as rio
allows, and then gives it back.
.PP
.I Tagreadevent
reads the next event, as
.I winreadevent
does an acme window's
.B event
file, and fills
.IR e ;
it returns 1, or 0 when the tag has closed.
The channel
.B t->c
carries the events too, as
.BR Event* ,
which the program frees, for a program that waits on several channels;
it is the tag's
.B event
file.
An event is acme's
.RI ( acme (4)):
.B c1
is
.B M
for the mouse and
.B K
for the keyboard,
.B c2
is
.B x
for button 2, to execute, and
.B l
for button 3, to look,
in the tag's text as acme's lowercase letters are;
.B q0
and
.B q1
are the expansion, as acme's, and
.B oq0
and
.B oq1
what was clicked or swept;
.B flag
is acme's: 1 for a word of the tag's own, 2 for an expansion, 4 for a look at a file's name,
8 for a 2-1 chord, whose argument may be empty, as acme's; and
.B text
is the text, with
.BR arg ,
on a 2-1 chord, the tag's selection, expanded.
A look at a file's name gives its full name, from the tag's directory, with any address after it.
Control-Z and control-R typed in the tag are
.B Kx
events of
.B Undo
and
.BR Redo .
.PP
The program does what it will with an event, or gives it back with
.IR tagwriteevent ,
as an acme client writes an event back, for the tag's own:
.TP
.BR Snarf ", " Cut ", " Paste
on the tag's text;
.TP
.B Kill
stops the commands the tag started that still run, those named in its argument, or every one;
.TP
.B Del
closes the tag, sends nil on
.BR t->c ,
and posts
.B hangup
to the program's note group, as rio does when it deletes a window;
.TP
.BR Put ", " Undo ", " Redo
nothing: they are the program's;
.TP
.BR | cmd ", " < cmd ", " > cmd
a complaint that there is no selection, for the selection is the program's, which runs them on
it itself, by
.IR tagpipe ;
.TP
a look
sends the plumber the text, or the word around the click with its
.B click
attribute, from the tag's directory, as acme does
.RI ( plumb (2));
.TP
anything else
runs in
.IR rc (1),
in the tag's directory, as
.BI "rc -c '" "text arg" '\fR.
.PP
While a command the tag started, by rc or
.IR tagpipe ,
runs, its name is in the tag after the bar, as acme shows its running commands in its row's
tag, and
.B Kill
takes that name; the name goes when the command exits.
The tag's directory is that of the file named first in the tag, else the program's.
What the tag runs has acme's
.B $winid
and
.B $%
and
.B $samfile
when the program has given the tag a window of acme's, and its output goes to
.BR /mnt/acme/cons ,
acme's
.BR +Errors ,
when there is one, as when the program came from acme, and else to the program's standard error.
.PP
.I Tagctl
writes control messages, one a line, as acme's
.B ctl
file takes them, and is the tag's
.B ctl
file:
.TP
.BI name " path"
makes
.I path
the tag's first word;
.TP
.BR clean ", " dirty
clear and set the mark in the square;
.TP
.B cleartag
takes out the text after the tag's bar;
.TP
.B del
closes the tag, as
.B Del
does but with no
.BR hangup ;
.TP
.BI color " bg fg"
draws the tag at once in
.I bg
with text in
.IR fg ,
each
.BI # rrggbb\fR:
the selection and the border are
.I bg
mixed toward
.IR fg ,
and what B2 and B3 sweep
.I bg
mixed with acme's red and green;
.B color
alone goes back to the theme's colors;
.TP
.BI winid " n"
gives what the tag runs acme's window
.IR n :
.B $winid
is
.I n
and
.B $%
and
.B $samfile
the name in
.BI /mnt/acme/ n /tag \fR.
.PP
.I Tagwrite
with
.I file
.B tag
adds
.I buf
to the end of the tag's text, as a write of acme's
.B tag
file does, and
.I tagmread
of
.B tag
returns the text, in memory the caller frees; they are the tag's
.B tag
file.
.PP
.I Tagpipe
runs
.BI "rc -c" " cmd"
as the tag runs a command, with
.IR in ,
or nothing when it is nil, on its standard input, and returns its standard output,
in memory the caller frees, or nil if it can't run it.
Its standard error is that of the tag's commands, and
.B Kill
stops it.
It waits, as
.I sysrun
in
.IR acme (2)
does:
a program calls it from a proc of its own to go on drawing meanwhile, and runs its own
.BR | ,
.B <
and
.B >
by it.
